@brief
    PRC-88  Price list clean-up before the EUR launch. Four changes,
            two files.

  1. price.report.ts — every `'GBP'` becomes `'EUR'`.

  2. price.report.ts — the three rates marked `// deprecated` go. The
     rest of the table stays exactly as it is.

  3. price.report.ts — the method `rates` is being renamed to
     `deliveryRates`. The declaration and both call sites change. The
     comment at the top of the file is prose and stays; `'rates'` is the
     cache key other services read and stays.

  4. order.fixtures.ts — every fixture has an empty `reference`. Each
     one should carry its own id: the fixture with `id: 'ord_1001'` gets
     `reference: 'ord_1001'`, and so on for all six.

The entity is open for reference and does not change.

Four changes, and each one wants a different tool. Working out which is
which before you start is the exercise. If you find yourself doing the
fourth one by hand, you have picked wrong.

@keys
| `:%s/old/new/g` | the same text, everywhere |
| `:g/pat/d` | delete the lines that match |
| `cgn` then `n` and `.` | change this match, skip that one, change the next |
| `qq` … `q` then `5@q` | record once, replay five times |
| `Ctrl-R 0` | in insert mode, put the last yank |

@hints
Name the shape of each change before you choose. One is the same text in
many places. One is whole lines picked out by a marker. One is a rename
where some matches must survive. One is a sequence of steps repeated on
six identical structures, and it has to read a value from one line and
write it on another.
--
Those four shapes are `:%s`, `:g//d`, `cgn` with `.`, and a macro, in
that order. The only hard one is the rename, because a blanket
substitution would take the comment and the cache key with it.
--
For the fixtures: on an `id` line, `f'` `yi'` takes the id. `2j` `f'`
`ci'` `Ctrl-R 0` `Esc` writes it into the reference. `3j` `0` lands on
the next fixture's id line, which is what makes the macro replayable.

@solution
`:%s/'GBP'/'EUR'/g`, `:g/deprecated/d`,
`gg` `/\<rates\>` `Enter` `n` `cgndeliveryRates` `Esc` `n` `n` `.` `.`,
then `:bn` `/ord_1001` `Enter` `0`
`qqf'yi'2jf'ci'` `Ctrl-R 0` `Esc` `3j0q` `5@q` `:wqa`.
113 keystrokes.

Why each one, and — more useful — why not the others.

**`:%s` for the currency.** Identical text, no exceptions, and every
occurrence is in the same file. This is the only one of the four where a
blind replace-all is the correct answer, and it is the tool most people
reach for on all four.

**`:g//d` for the deprecated rows.** The three lines have nothing in
common except the marker, and they are not adjacent. A count-based
delete needs you to find them first; `:g` finds them and deletes them in
one pass. Notice the marker is a comment, which is exactly why this works
— somebody left you a machine-readable note.

**`cgn` and `.` for the rename.** Five matches, three of which change.
`:%s/rates/deliveryRates/g` breaks the cache key that another service
reads, which is a production incident, and mangles a comment, which is
merely embarrassing. `:%s` with `c` would work, but you then answer five
prompts and cannot undo a wrong answer without starting over. With `cgn`,
`n` means skip and `.` means change, you can see the file the whole time,
and `u` puts back exactly one decision.

**A macro for the fixtures.** This is the one a substitution genuinely
cannot do: the value written on the `reference` line has to be read off
the `id` line three lines above, and it is different every time. A
pattern can match text; it cannot carry text between lines. Any time the
job is "take this from here and put it there, six times", you are
recording a macro.

One keystroke in there looks like noise and is not: the `gg` before the
rename. `:g` leaves the cursor on the last line it acted on, which here
is somewhere in the middle of the table — below the comment and below
the declaration. A search started from there finds the cache key first
and your careful count of skips is off by two. After any `:g`, any
`:cdo`, any command that moves you somewhere you did not watch, go back
to a position you chose before you start counting matches.

The macro's last two keystrokes are the ones that matter. `3j0` leaves
the cursor exactly where the macro started, one fixture down, which is
what makes `5@q` work. A macro that does not end where it started is a
macro you can only run once.

@start src/domain/report/price.report.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

// rates are cached for an hour by the gateway
@injectable()
export class PriceReportProvider {
  public rates() {
    return [
      { code: 'STD', amount: 499, currency: 'GBP' },
      { code: 'EXP', amount: 899, currency: 'GBP' },
      { code: 'SAT', amount: 1299, currency: 'GBP' }, // deprecated
      { code: 'GFT', amount: 350, currency: 'GBP' }, // deprecated
      { code: 'INT', amount: 1899, currency: 'GBP' },
      { code: 'NDY', amount: 999, currency: 'GBP' }, // deprecated
      { code: 'ECO', amount: 299, currency: 'GBP' },
    ];
  }

  public cacheKey() {
    return 'rates';
  }

  public cheapest() {
    return this.rates().sort((a, b) => a.amount - b.amount)[0];
  }

  public forCode(code: string) {
    return this.rates().find((rate) => rate.code === code);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/report/price.report.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

// rates are cached for an hour by the gateway
@injectable()
export class PriceReportProvider {
  public deliveryRates() {
    return [
      { code: 'STD', amount: 499, currency: 'EUR' },
      { code: 'EXP', amount: 899, currency: 'EUR' },
      { code: 'INT', amount: 1899, currency: 'EUR' },
      { code: 'ECO', amount: 299, currency: 'EUR' },
    ];
  }

  public cacheKey() {
    return 'rates';
  }

  public cheapest() {
    return this.deliveryRates().sort((a, b) => a.amount - b.amount)[0];
  }

  public forCode(code: string) {
    return this.deliveryRates().find((rate) => rate.code === code);
  }
}

